WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.050 --> 00:00:09.140
I like to ask questions that I think are deep
and difficult and beautiful.

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:14.309
The esthetic sense of what makes a question
elegant and beautiful matters a lot to me.

00:00:14.309 --> 00:00:18.730
And then I try to pick problems where I can
make progress.

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:22.029
Some problems are beautiful, but I impossibly
out there.

00:00:22.029 --> 00:00:27.529
So I do feel that the way you build your great
edifices is by putting bricks in place.

00:00:27.529 --> 00:00:31.630
The thing is, very often you can actually
do that for things that you would think would

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:33.420
be impossibly difficult.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:36.670
So it's a matter of just being creative and
bringing to bear tools that you've learned

00:00:36.670 --> 00:00:41.489
from different fields and putting them together
in a different way.

00:00:41.489 --> 00:00:46.449
I'm a theorist and I work in sort of broadly
across multiple areas of physics, theoretical

00:00:46.449 --> 00:00:47.840
neuroscience and computer science.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:51.699
So, you know, in my daily work, I move smoothly
between all these fields.

00:00:51.699 --> 00:00:54.350
I'm doing them all at the same time.

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:59.320
My Ph.D. and my training was in, you know,
the fundamental laws of nature the nature

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:05.960
of space and time, gravity and black holes
and the early universe and how you quantize

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:07.080
the theory of gravity.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:09.190
So that's a core component.

00:01:09.190 --> 00:01:13.290
What I do is thinking about, you know, basically
the foundations of physics and the fundamentals

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:14.850
of what space and time are.

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:20.520
They've clearly been interested throughout
my career in the nature of information, what

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:26.630
it is and how it appears and is processed
by systems in the natural world.

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:31.700
I've been interested in questions of the one
hand like black holes and do they destroy

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:32.700
information?

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:34.500
Basically, Hawking's famous paradox, you know,
how is that possible?

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:39.840
On the other hand, I've been interested in
questions like how can neural systems take

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.220
information from the world and code it and
then transmit it to the central brain.

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:48.740
There's another clearly a chain that's been
running through my work, which which has to

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:50.710
do with the notion of complexity.

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:55.270
I've written a whole series of papers at various
points about how some kinds of physical dynamics

00:01:55.270 --> 00:02:00.660
produce complex states of matter and others
don't.

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:05.600
Basically going after the question about how
physical and biological processes can produce

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:09.670
complexity from states that weren't complex
to start with.

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:14.640
So overall, you know, I'm interested in the
laws and principles that govern the organization

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:19.250
of systems in nature, whether they be living
or non-living And so I sort of tend to spend

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:21.130
a rather enormous range of scales.

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:28.530
I guess while there is a lot of talk of interdisciplinarity
in academia, in practice, In fact, it's a

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:32.470
it's somewhat more rigid than that because
basically you get a degree in a field.

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:37.630
You are rewarded for performance in a field
by the standards of that field.

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:44.291
So navigating the sort of doing multiple things
that kind of career requires some care in

00:02:44.291 --> 00:02:48.060
the sense that you have to establish street
cred in the disciplines separately.

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:53.069
The people in each field accept that you're
worthy of the standard of being an expert

00:02:53.069 --> 00:02:54.069
in that field.

00:02:54.069 --> 00:02:56.990
Maybe knowledge doesn't have to be cut that
way.

00:02:56.990 --> 00:03:04.240
When I was seven, we were living in Calcutta
in India, and we used to go to the market

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:09.850
and they have these booksellers I saw how
and why Wonder book The Lives of Famous Scientists.

00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:13.040
I remember thinking, Wow, you can do this.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:14.140
It just seemed amazing, right?

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:19.880
I mean, these people just sat around and figured
out what the world was like.

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:21.010
Physics as a discipline.

00:03:21.010 --> 00:03:23.680
I used to see it anyway as the most fundamental,
right?

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:26.600
It's the basic laws from which everything
else is built.

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:31.069
So it was clear I'd be a physicist, but I
think as I began to go through high school

00:03:31.069 --> 00:03:35.099
and learn to program machines and stuff like
that, that occurred to me that if computers

00:03:35.099 --> 00:03:39.319
that I could program were constrained in what
they could do by the nature of the circuits

00:03:39.319 --> 00:03:41.270
they have, then surely I am, too.

00:03:41.270 --> 00:03:45.700
So I began to feel that there was a problem
with my understanding of the laws of nature,

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:49.300
and that I clearly needed to understand how
were able to think the thoughts that we're

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:50.520
able to think.

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:54.209
I was a junior fellow of something called
the Harvard Society Fellows, which is not

00:03:54.209 --> 00:03:55.900
affiliated any particular lab.

00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:57.180
You could do whatever you want.

00:03:57.180 --> 00:04:01.489
So I use that freedom to sort of moonlight
in neuroscience labs and basically learn the

00:04:01.489 --> 00:04:02.489
subject.

00:04:02.489 --> 00:04:06.400
And by the time you know how to master a different
toolkit, I would say than most people.

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:11.750
I knew lots of things in theoretical physics
and do lots of math and tons of computer science.

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:16.739
One of my first papers as a graduate student
occurred because I had just learned general

00:04:16.739 --> 00:04:20.130
relativity and therefore knew how to work.

00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:23.400
Describe the mathematics of differential geometry
and curved spaces.

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:27.530
And at the same time I had been thinking about
the question of Occam's Razor.

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:32.270
Is there a mathematical reason why a simple
explanation in some sense is actually a better

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:34.509
description of a phenomenon?

00:04:34.509 --> 00:04:39.289
And I started thinking about the geometry
of the space of mathematical models as explanations

00:04:39.289 --> 00:04:42.900
using the tools I just learned by studying
general relativity.

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:46.069
That was my first paper as a graduate student
and it was very well cited and all that kind

00:04:46.069 --> 00:04:47.069
of stuff.

00:04:47.069 --> 00:04:50.199
And I really enjoyed that because it was just
putting together pieces that people hadn't

00:04:50.199 --> 00:04:56.729
really put together before and kind of assembling
these all into a story.

00:04:56.729 --> 00:05:01.129
I have many tools in the toolkit, so with
my students and post-docs, we've been basically

00:05:01.129 --> 00:05:02.360
deploying those tools.

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:04.969
When we were thinking about the circuits in
the brain, there's something called the grid

00:05:04.969 --> 00:05:06.189
cell system.

00:05:06.189 --> 00:05:08.370
Make the maps that you have of the world.

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:13.849
We realized that the self-organizing dynamics
of this neural circuit is an analogy of classic

00:05:13.849 --> 00:05:17.550
self-organizing problems in condensed matter
physics, in particular something called the

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:21.259
frontal control of a model that we were able
to take and say, Oh, great, but what we need

00:05:21.259 --> 00:05:23.370
is a two dimensional generalization of this
thing.

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:28.620
So once we realized that we were able to guess
how to organize a system of neurons, to have

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:32.599
a dynamics to produce these kinds of hierarchical
maps of the world.

00:05:32.599 --> 00:05:39.059
So the idea came directly from knowing some
problems and convinced modern physics For

00:05:39.059 --> 00:05:42.589
me, literature matters a great deal.

00:05:42.589 --> 00:05:45.969
You know, I spent much of my childhood summers
reading books.

00:05:45.969 --> 00:05:50.360
My imagination was basically formed by reading
these stories.

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:53.599
The liberal arts really are getting short
shrift in our world today.

00:05:53.599 --> 00:05:59.219
I think it's extremely dangerous because I
don't think of science and technology teach

00:05:59.219 --> 00:06:00.509
us how to live.

00:06:00.509 --> 00:06:07.849
I think the liberal arts teach us how to live
the dangers of becoming unmoored from our

00:06:07.849 --> 00:06:08.849
cultures.

00:06:08.849 --> 00:06:13.180
And our histories that our pasts are on ample
display around the world.

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:17.360
If there isn't a healthy society to support
science, science will die.

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:20.619
Even the history of science and sociology
of science matters.

00:06:20.619 --> 00:06:25.539
We often have this heroic narrative of the
world was in darkness, and then great Person

00:06:25.539 --> 00:06:27.999
X came along and had this deep inside.

00:06:27.999 --> 00:06:30.509
And then all of a sudden all was good and
we knew something.

00:06:30.509 --> 00:06:32.370
That's not the way science works.

00:06:32.370 --> 00:06:36.930
There's the sea of developments that made
that possible, and that matters.

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:42.979
In my own teaching, what I do is I try to
use literary examples and analogies every

00:06:42.979 --> 00:06:45.360
week in addition to the sort of science stuff.

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:50.779
We would keep aside half of one class to read
something from literature and something to

00:06:50.779 --> 00:06:52.699
contextualize.

00:06:52.699 --> 00:06:56.490
So this is in the first chapter of Swann's
Way, the first book of the Remembrance of

00:06:56.490 --> 00:06:58.499
Things Past by Marcel Proust.

00:06:58.499 --> 00:07:06.169
So he says, "For many years already, everything
about Combray had ceased to exist for me when

00:07:06.169 --> 00:07:10.810
one day in winter, as I returned home, my
mother, seeing that I was cold, suggested

00:07:10.810 --> 00:07:13.289
that I have a little tea.

00:07:13.289 --> 00:07:17.240
She sent for one of those squat plump cakes
called Petite Madeleine.

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:20.999
That look as though they have been molded
in the grooved valve of a scallop shell.

00:07:20.999 --> 00:07:26.870
But at that very instant, when the mouthful
of tea mixed with cake crumbs touched my palate,

00:07:26.870 --> 00:07:28.649
I quivered.

00:07:28.649 --> 00:07:30.389
Suddenly the memory appeared.

00:07:30.389 --> 00:07:36.110
That taste was the taste of the little piece
of Madeleine, which on Sunday mornings had

00:07:36.110 --> 00:07:42.449
come to my Aunt Leonie would give me after
dipping in her infusion of tea or lime blossom.

00:07:42.449 --> 00:07:49.809
I think it's just a remarkable document about
the power of taste and smell to evoke memories.

00:07:49.809 --> 00:07:54.139
Silence is great, but, you know, every time
you walk around, it's good to know what's

00:07:54.139 --> 00:07:59.050
around you, what made the world, what makes
the world and sustains the world is the culture

00:07:59.050 --> 00:08:00.389
and society, the people around you.

